<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1106">1106</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf ibn Tashfin was the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> who died in 1106. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> as the Almoravid capital, completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and, at the invitation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings, intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Sagrajas</a> in 1086. He later deposed the taifa rulers and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> under direct Almoravid rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin.jpg" alt="Yusuf ibn Tashfin" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yusuf_Ben_Tasfin_dinar_22562.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> of the Banu Turgut, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamtuna">Lamtuna</a>, a tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> confederation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-umar">Abu Bakr ibn Umar</a> led the Lamtuna as chief commander after his brother Yahya was killed in 1056, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sous">Sous</a> and its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a>, and entrusted his cousin Yusuf with Sous and the northern provinces. Yusuf's standing grew so far that Abu Bakr gave him his wife, Zaynab an-Nafzawiyyah, and judged recapture of the post politically unfeasible. In 1061 Yusuf took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-al-muslimin">Amir al-Muslimin</a>, recognising the Abbasid caliph as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yusuf built an army of Sudanese contingents, Christian mercenaries and Saharan tribes. It crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1075, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oujda">Oujda</a> in 1079, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1080 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1083, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenes">Ténès</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> in 1082-83. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, begun in 1070, his capital in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a>. In 1085 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and took tribute from Muslim princes, and al-Mu'tamid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> called on Yusuf, saying he would rather be a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-driver in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> than a swineherd in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><p>On 23 October 1086 Yusuf, with about 15,000 men and 10,000 Andalusian fighters, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a>, but the death of his heir sent him back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. When he returned in 1090 he failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, and he judged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings lax and exiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and Málaga to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a>, followed by al-Mu'tamid a year later. All the Muslim lands of the peninsula but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> came under him. His forces defeated Alfonso at Consuegra in 1097, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>'s death in 1099 a seven-month siege led by Mazdali ibn Tilankan gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Medieval writers described Yusuf as wise and shrewd, suited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and uninterested in Andalusian court pomp. He spoke Arabic poorly. He died in 1106 and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf">Ali ibn Yusuf</a> succeeded him. Andalusian revolt began from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> about 1119, Christian pressure grew in the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, from about 1120, engulfed the south, so that his territories broke up under his successors Ibrahim ibn Tashfin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishaq-ibn-ali">Ishaq ibn Ali</a>, the last Almoravid rulers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf ibn Tashfin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Marrakesh, founded around 1070 (traditionally 1062), became the capital of the northern branch under <b>Yusuf ibn Tashfin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Badajoz, the Almoravid army of <b>Yusuf ibn Tashfin</b>, called in by the taifa rulers of al-Andalus, shattered the forces of King Alfonso VI of Castile.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yusuf ibn Tashfin</b> took Fez around 1069 to 1070, ending Maghrawa rule in Morocco.</blockquote><a href="/state/maghrawa-emirate-of-fez">Maghrawa emirate of Fez</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamim joined the Almoravid campaign of 1086 but, like other taifa kings, was distrusted by their emir <b>Yusuf ibn Tashfin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-taifa-of-malaga">Second Taifa of Málaga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Marrakech, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.63,-7.98&amp;year=1096" aria-label="Marrakech on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4344 1012 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Yusuf ibn Tashfin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4794 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.63,-7.98&amp;year=1096">What was here in 1096? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
